      "Languages differ strikingly in how they divide the spectrum of color. Some have only a handful of basic color terms; others, such as Russian, distinguish by name between shades that English speakers group together, treating light blue and dark blue as different colors rather than as varieties of one. For much of the twentieth century, such differences were taken as evidence that language shapes perception, so that speakers of different languages literally see the world differently.",
      "In 1969, however, the anthropologists Brent Berlin and Paul Kay challenged this view. Comparing dozens of languages, they argued that color vocabularies are far from arbitrary: when a language has only two basic terms, they correspond roughly to light and dark; when it acquires a third, it is almost always red; and further terms are added in a largely predictable order. This regularity, they suggested, reflected the shared biology of human vision rather than the peculiarities of particular cultures.",
      "The debate did not end there. Later critics pointed out that Berlin and Kay had relied heavily on translators and dictionaries, and that their sample overrepresented written languages. Meanwhile, laboratory studies produced a more modest conclusion than either side had originally proposed. Russian speakers, for instance, distinguish light from dark blue slightly faster than English speakers do, but only when the two shades fall on opposite sides of a Russian color boundary, and the advantage disappears when the speakers are distracted by a simultaneous verbal task.",
