      "For most of the history of psychology, forgetting was treated as a failure, a leak in the vessel of memory, to be minimized by rehearsal and mnemonic tricks. The assumption was intuitive: an ideal memory, one imagines, would retain everything. Yet the rare individuals who come closest to that ideal tend to describe it less as a gift than as a burden. Unable to discard the trivial, they report being overwhelmed by detail and struggling to form the generalizations on which ordinary reasoning depends.",
      "Such cases have encouraged a reappraisal. A growing body of research suggests that forgetting is not merely the passive decay of unused information but, at least in part, an active process that the brain carries out deliberately. In studies of mice, for instance, the formation of new neurons in the hippocampus appears to accelerate the loss of older memories, as if the brain were clearing space. Far from being a defect, forgetting on this account is a form of maintenance.",
      "The functional argument runs roughly as follows. An organism that remembered every instance of every experience would be poorly equipped to deal with a changing world, since outdated information would compete on equal terms with current information. By allowing the specific to fade while preserving the general, forgetting enables the abstraction that makes past experience useful in new situations. One remembers that the stove is hot, not every occasion on which one approached it.",
      "Proponents of this view are careful not to overstate it. Pathological forgetting, as in dementia, is plainly harmful, and the active mechanisms they describe have been demonstrated far more convincingly in mice than in humans. Their claim is narrower: that a memory system built for making decisions, rather than for keeping archives, would be expected to forget, and that ours, accordingly, does.",
